 to take one mustard  per person and there's just  none  for centuries french cuisine has used  mustard in virtually everything today  the french consume on average a kilo of  the stuff per person per year so you can  imagine the reaction when grocery store  shelves began to look like this  it wasn't long before desperation led to  hoarding so they've been rationing  essentially they've been trying to  limit the number of bottles people can  buy per visit  they've been very careful because they  know that things are tight  call it a french tragedy albeit with  canadian roots let me explain you see  while it's widely known that canada is  the top exporter of maple syrup in the  world we're also number one in potash  flax lentils blueberries  and yes mustard you could say nobody  cuts the mustard without canada there's  no cartel and mustard like there's a  cartel in maple syrup but not in mustard  even if there was there's nothing a  mustard cartel could have done about the  droughts that led to a 50 drop in  mustard seed production last year  french importers didn't panic because  they knew they could make up the  shortfall with seeds grown in russia and  ukraine  or so they thought  the good news is that canada's 2022  crops look promising so the shortage  should end in 2023  until then the french will have to  endure some online needling  i wonder how they'll take it oh wait i  know  hold the mustard  mike drollate global news toronto  and that is global national for this  saturday night i'm nithu garcia  tonight's year canada is french creek  harbor in parksville bc we love seeing  your canada please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope you'll join us  again tomorrow have a great night  [Music]
 

